": -1.06}, "rating_margin": -5.6, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.21, "baseline_margin": -3.28, "analyst_adjustment": -1.22, "key_factors": ["R+5 district lean with incumbent Jeff Hurd running for a second term", "Hurd's large financial advantage ($1.8M cash vs. $84K for Romero)", "Raters lean Republican (Likely/Lean R consensus), though two call it a Tossup", "Markets price Hurd around 70-72%, with only one usable poll (July, D-sponsored, Hurd +1)"], "rationale": "The district's R+5 lean, Hurd's incumbency and dominant fundraising outweigh a single July poll showing a one-point race and Democratic targeting. Markets and most raters point to a Hurd win, but the closeness of that poll and split rater views keep this short of Likely R.", "watch": "Fresh public polling after early voting begins; if Romero stays within a couple of points, this moves toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 76 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Hurd (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Jeff Hurd is seeking a second term in a Western Slope district that leans Republican by about five points. He enters the fall with a major financial edge, having raised $3.6 million and holding $1.8 million on hand, while Democrat Dwayne Romero has raised $717,000 with modest cash remaining. National attention from both parties signals the race is competitive, and headlines show Hurd defending his record as independent while Romero challenges it.\n\nThe evidence points to a Hurd advantage but not a safe one. Most expert raters place the race in the Likely or Lean Republican column, though two call it a Tossup. The only recent poll, a Democratic-sponsored survey from July, showed Hurd ahead by just one point. Prediction markets price Hurd's chances around 70 percent. The race sits in the Lean Republican range, with genuine room for movement given thin polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2665, "margin": -3.72}, {"date": "